Let $\mathcal{A}$ denote the set of all symmetric idempotent matrices on the real vector space $\mathbb{R}^n$; that is, $\mathcal{A} = \{A \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times n} : A^\top = A \text{ and } A^2 = A\}$. Let $I$ be the $n\times n$ identity matrix and $0$ the $n\times n$ zero matrix. A fuzzy relation $s: \mathcal{A} \times \mathcal{A} \to [0,1]$ is called an $s$-map if the following hold:

(i) $s(I, I) = 1$;

(ii) for all $A, B \in \mathcal{A}$ with $AB = BA = 0$, $s(A, B) = 0$;

(iii) for all $A, B \in \mathcal{A}$ with $AB = BA = 0$ and every $C \in \mathcal{A}$,
$$s(A+B, C) = s(A, C) + s(B, C) \quad \text{and} \quad s(C, A+B) = s(C, A) + s(C, B).$$

Determine whether every $s$-map is necessarily symmetric; that is, must $s(A, B) = s(B, A)$ hold for all $A, B \in \mathcal{A}$?

